#!/usr/bin/env bash
# For one person running coding agents on their own machine.
#
# Answers four questions, in about a minute, with no setup, no database, no cgroups and
# no model requests spent:
#
#   1. How much RAM is each agent session actually using? (RSS *and* PSS -- see below)
#   2. How much of the last 7 days was my agent actually WORKING, versus sitting open?
#   3. How long does a fresh agent process take to become ready?
#   4. Did an agent leave anything running that I have forgotten about?
#
# Question 2 is the one nobody measures about themselves and the one most likely to
# surprise you.
#
# Nothing here writes, deletes or sends anything. Question 3 starts agent processes and
# kills them at their readiness event, so it spends no model requests.
set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" || exit 2
I=instruments

say() { printf '\n\033[1m=== %s ===\033[0m\n' "$1"; }

say "1. What is each session using? (RSS and PSS)"
cat <<'NOTE'
Read the PSS column, not RSS, if you want to know what another session would cost you.
An agent CLI is typically one large compiled binary, so its pages are SHARED between
concurrent sessions: RSS counts those once per session, PSS counts them once. If the two
numbers are far apart, that gap is the shared binary.
NOTE
python3 "$I/measure_session_footprint.py" "$@" || true

say "2. How much of the last 7 days was an agent actually working?"
cat <<'NOTE'
Measured from your own transcripts: a session "owns" a minute if it wrote anything in it.
busy_minutes is out of the whole 7-day window, INCLUDING the minutes nothing happened --
that is the point, and dividing by busy minutes instead would delete the very thing this
measures.
NOTE
python3 "$I/measure_active_concurrency.py" "$@" || true

say "3. How long does a fresh agent process take to be ready?"
cat <<'NOTE'
Two things worth knowing before you read this: "ready" is the FIRST event in the stream,
and tools are typically still connecting at that point -- so this is a floor, not the cost
of a useful turn. And on a loaded machine these numbers move by 2-3x between runs, so look
at every repetition rather than the median.
NOTE
REPS="${REPS:-3}" python3 "$I/bench_cold_start.py" --skip-turn || true

say "4. Did an agent leave anything running?"
cat <<'NOTE'
On an ordinary local machine there are no per-session cgroups, so this falls back to
walking process parentage and says so loudly. In that mode its answer is a FLOOR: it
cannot see a process that was backgrounded with `&` and reparented away. It demonstrates
that blindness on itself rather than asking you to take it on trust.
NOTE
python3 "$I/census_background_work.py" "$@"
rc=$?
case "$rc" in
  0) echo; echo "(cgroup mode: the zero above is a real count)";;
  4) echo; echo "(degraded mode: the count above is a FLOOR -- see the warning)";;
  3) echo; echo "(the self-test FAILED, so do not believe the count above)";;
esac

say "Done"
cat <<'NOTE'
What this did NOT measure: token cost, latency under concurrency, or output quality --
only what a session costs to HOLD. Limits: docs/limits.md.
If you are sizing a worker pool rather than checking your own laptop, run
./can-i-pool-this instead.
NOTE
